// All 36 games of the FIBA Women's Basketball World Cup 2026.
// Structure and tip-off times come from FIBA's official game schedule
// (frozen in scripts/official.mjs, which is the authority); event ids,
// arenas and scores come from ESPN's basketball/fiba scoreboard.
// Regenerate with: npm run fetch:tournament
//
// `ko` is the tip-off instant as an ISO 8601 string. Every game of this
// edition is played in Berlin, and Berlin is on CEST (+02:00) for the
// whole 4-13 September window, so unlike the multi-venue football siblings
// this tournament genuinely has ONE offset. The offset is still written out
// explicitly so `new Date(ko)` resolves to the right absolute instant and
// can be formatted into any viewer's timezone.
//
// `ko` is null and `tbdTip` is true on the games whose tip-off FIBA has not
// announced yet: the sheet prints "17:45 or 20:45" for the qualification
// round and "16:30 or 20:00" for the semi-finals, to be confirmed when the
// previous round ends. Those carry `date` (the Berlin calendar date, which
// IS known) so the schedule can still place them, and render the time as TBC.
//
// `label1`/`label2` are the bracket placeholders a final-phase game was
// drawn with ("Winner Group A"). They stay alongside the resolved teams so
// the bracket can show a slot's provenance and so an undecided edition
// renders from the same records.
//
// `ot` counts OVERTIME PERIODS (0 in regulation). Basketball has no draw:
// a finished game always has a winner, which is why there is no aet/pens
// pair here and why the bracket can always resolve a completed game.
//
// `tv` lists the US platforms carrying the game, from Warner Bros.
// Discovery's published table (frozen in scripts/official.mjs), NOT from
// ESPN's broadcast field. HBO Max and DAZN (Courtside 1891) carry all 36
// games; only the linear channel varies. `tvNote` carries a round-level
// window WBD announced without saying which game of the round gets it.
//
// `espnId` is the ESPN event id, used to fetch that game's box score on
// demand and to match the live overlay. It is null on a final-phase game
// ESPN has not published yet.

